{
  "reason": "Select only model_design because the dilation schedule is model-owned and all data, training, and evaluation components are frozen. The current gated depthwise NextItNet achieves validation HR@10 of 0.625; its 121-position theoretical receptive field motivates testing shorter temporal offsets given approximately 10.7 training interactions per user, although the mean alone does not establish a bottleneck. Historical gating and residual-scaling successes support controlled architectural experiments, but their different parent architectures do not justify adding gates, weight sharing, or residual scaling to this schedule-only ablation.",
  "module": ["model_design"],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": null,
    "model_design": "In NextItNetModel.__init__, replace the existing schedule assignment with dilations = [1, 2] * max(1, math.ceil(num_blocks / 2)), producing [1,2,1,2,1,2,1,2] for the existing eight independent blocks while supporting the num_blocks argument. With two kernel-size-3 causal convolutions per block, this reduces the theoretical receptive field from 121 to 49 positions and tests whether emphasizing nearby transitions improves sparse-history recommendations. Preserve self.blocks, all existing variable names and constructor, forward, encode_sequence, and score_candidates signatures, along with depthwise and pointwise convolutions, value gating, positional and item embeddings, normalization, activation, dropout, padding masks, residual connections, initialization, last-nonpadding selection, and tied-embedding scoring. Keep MAXLEN=128, HIDDEN_UNITS=64, NUM_BLOCKS=8, KERNEL_SIZE=3, DROPOUT_RATE=0.2, LR=0.001, WEIGHT_DECAY=0.0, and BATCH_SIZE=128, retaining exactly the three permitted imports, eight numeric constants, and two classes with methods only. Allocate one of the 44 candidate slots for the unchanged host to compare validation HR@10 against 0.625 using training seed 2024, validation sampling seed 42, identical splits and candidate sets, and unchanged preprocessing, BPR loss, Adam, clipping, training negatives, epochs, evaluation, and checkpoint selection; treat 0.635, or two additional hits among 200 validation users, as an experimental target, with no test-metric access, ensembling, or model-side file/network access, printing, training, evaluation, or CSV generation.",
    "training_evaluation": null
  }
}
